<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augusto Pinochet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augusto Pinochet was a Chilean general who led the military coup of 11 September 1973 that overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a>. He ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> as dictator until 1990, with widespread repression and free-market economic reforms, and imposed the constitution of 1980. After losing the plebiscite of 1988 he handed power to an elected president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet.jpg" alt="Augusto Pinochet" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ministerio de Relaciones Exteriores de Chile · CC BY 2.0 cl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augusto_Pinochet_foto_oficial(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Augusto José Ramón Pinochet Ugarte was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> on 25 November 1915 to a middle-class family. His father, Augusto Pinochet Vera, descended from an 18th-century Breton immigrant, and his mother, Avelina Ugarte Martínez, was of Basque heritage. He attended schools in Valparaíso and Quillota and entered the Military School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> in 1931. His records show an average student, and in 1935 he graduated as a second lieutenant in the infantry. After postings to regiments in Concepción, Valparaíso and Iquique, he entered the Chilean War Academy, and he later wrote two books on military subjects, Geopolítica (1968) and Campaña de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a> (1972).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He became General Chief of Staff in early 1972 and was appointed commander-in-chief of the army by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a> on 23 August 1973. On 11 September 1973 the combined armed forces, including the Navy, Air Force and Carabineros, overthrew Allende's government, and the presidential palace, La Moneda, was shelled. A junta of four commanders was established; it suspended the Constitution and Congress, imposed censorship and a curfew, and banned the parties of Allende's coalition and then all others. Historians differ on the part played by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which tacitly supported the junta after the coup; some authors describe a covert campaign against Allende, while Sebastián Hurtado finds no documentary evidence of an active American role in the coup itself.</p><p>Pinochet was proclaimed Supreme Chief of the Nation on 17 June 1974 and officially titled President of the Republic on 17 December 1974. General Gustavo Leigh of the Air Force, who opposed him, was forced into retirement in 1978. The regime persecuted leftists and critics through executions, forced disappearances, internment and torture; a government commission report that took testimony from more than 30,000 people recorded at least 3,197 killed and about 29,000 tortured. Economists known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chicago-boys">Chicago Boys</a> guided a programme of market liberalisation. A plebiscite in 1980 approved a new constitution, and on 5 October 1988 voters rejected a further presidential term, which led to elections.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pinochet stepped down in 1990 but remained commander-in-chief of the army until 10 March 1998, when he became a senator for life. He was arrested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in October 1998 on an international warrant, the first time a former head of government was arrested on the principle of universal jurisdiction, and was released on medical grounds by the Home Secretary Jack Straw in March 2000. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, he faced about 300 criminal charges when he died of heart failure at the Military Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> on 10 December 2006, without having been convicted. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-senate">United States Senate</a> investigation in 2004 found a network of accounts at Riggs Bank that he and his associates used to move money.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Augusto Pinochet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Salvador Allende, elected in 1970 at the head of the Popular Unity coalition, was overthrown on 11 September 1973 by the armed forces under <b>Augusto Pinochet</b>, whose regime killed or caused the disappearance of more than 3,000 people and imprisoned and tortured tens of thousands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Officers seized power in Egypt in 1952, Brazil in 1964, Chile in 1973, when General <b>Augusto Pinochet</b> overthrew the elected government of Salvador Allende, and Argentina in 1976, and repeatedly in Nigeria, Ghana and many other African states; independence leaders banned rival parties; and personal rulers such as…</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>One member usually presides and often becomes head of state, and a strong president can turn a junta into a personal dictatorship, as <b>Augusto Pinochet</b> largely did in Chile.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Augusto Pinochet</b>’s rule in Chile used authoritarian means to secure investment, bringing growth and high inequality, and Suharto’s extirpation of the Communist Party of Indonesia allowed capitalism to expand there.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>In Latin America most nations were under military rule by the mid-1970s; <b>Augusto Pinochet</b> ruled Chile from 1973 to 1990.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>After a 1973 coup overthrew President Salvador Allende, a military junta under <b>Augusto Pinochet</b>, known for its violent suppression of opposition, gave the Chicago Boys, Chilean economists who had studied under Milton Friedman, significant political influence.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/neoliberalism">Neoliberalism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.05,-71.62&amp;year=1974" aria-label="Valparaíso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2716 3364 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Augusto Pinochet"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3166 3634h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3166 3634h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.05,-71.62&amp;year=1974">What was here in 1974? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
